B. NEW CONFIRMED DEFECTS
================================================================================
N1 (rank 8). THE PARTIAL-TOPOLOGY-ROUND DETECTOR CANNOT SEE AN ERROR RESPONSE. HIGH. 09_,11_,12_.
  A-2 added `topology-round-partial` so a half-answered topology poll is announced. Its test is
  `TopologyAndFlowMonitor.cpp:588-590`: switchesAnswered/hostsAnswered/linksAnswered are each
  `!body.empty()`. The HTTP status line is never consulted -- `noteAndAnnouncePollRound` is handed
  three std::strings (`:580`, called at `:681`) and nothing else.
  Measured, each arm >= 3 poll intervals (30 s each once converged):
      links    = HTTP 500 + JSON error body ....... partial did NOT fire (11_)
      switches = HTTP 500 ......................... partial did NOT fire (11_)
      hosts    = HTTP 500 ......................... partial did NOT fire (11_)
      links    = 200 + a JSON object, not an array  partial did NOT fire (12_)
      links    = 200 + non-JSON text .............. partial did NOT fire (12_)
      switches = EMPTY 200 body (MUTATION GATE) ... partial FIRED, 2 -> 3       (11_)
      links    = EMPTY 200 body (MUTATION GATE) ... partial FIRED, full text in 09_
  So the detector fires only when the body is EMPTY (a timeout or a refused connection). Any
  non-empty body -- which is what a 5xx from Ryu or the proxy actually looks like -- classifies
  the round COMPLETE. Throughout the 5xx arms the graph held nodes 14/14 up, edges 40/40 up, and
  /ndt/get_graph_data's top-level keys are exactly ["edges","nodes"]: no field anywhere says the
  round was partial. The only trace a 5xx /links leaves is 16 WARN lines
  "ignoring a links entry with no src/dst endpoint", and ZERO topology-fetch-failure warnings.
  CONTRAST INSIDE THE SAME BINARY (this is the fix spec): the flow-table fetch DOES branch on the
  outcome -- an HTTP 500 for one dpid is marked last_error=reported_failure and a non-JSON body is
  marked last_error=unparseable (09_, 10_). The topology poll is the one that does not.
  This closes round 1's X-1, which read "NOT OBSERVED -- the half-answer case was not constructed".
  It is now constructed, both ways: the case A-2 was written for is caught, and the case an actual
  failing controller produces is not.
  Recipe: point the kernel at a southbound that answers 500 for /v1.0/topology/links; watch for
  `topology-round-partial` in the kernel log for 100 s; then make the same endpoint return an
  empty 200 body and watch the warning appear.

N2 (from rank 10). THREE "UNREADABLE SWITCH" SHAPES, THREE BEHAVIOURS, ONLY TWO MARKED. MEDIUM.
  09_, 10_ (reproduced 2/2). One rule withdrawn fabric-wide while three switches answer badly:
      d1,d2,d7..d10  healthy 200 + array .......... swept to 3 rows.  No marks. CORRECT.
      d4             200 + EMPTY array ............ swept to 0 rows.  No marks. CORRECT (A-2's fix).
      d5             HTTP 500 .................... keeps the last good 4 rows, marked
                                                   last_error=reported_failure, stale_polls 1->7.
      d6             200 + non-JSON text ......... keeps the last good 4 rows, marked
                                                   last_error=unparseable, stale_polls 1->7.
      d3             200 + well-formed JSON whose per-dpid value is an OBJECT, not an array
                     -> the API republishes the junk verbatim and carries NO marker at all:
                        {"dpid": 3, "flows": {"3": {"unexpected": "object"}}}
  So a consumer that does `flows["3"]` as a list gets a type error (or, if lenient, "zero rules"),
  with nothing in the response to say the switch was not read. The two neighbouring shapes both
  keep the last good snapshot and both say so. Recovery is fine: with the southbound healthy again
  all ten converge to 3 rows within 15 s (09_ phase D).

N3 (from rank 3). A DEAD SWITCH'S FLOW TABLE IS SERVED UNMARKED FOR THE FIRST ~10-12 s. MEDIUM. 05_.
  s6 killed out of band (`sudo -n ndtwin-p4-power off s6`; ps on the exact pid = gone, :30056
  closed). /ndt/get_switch_openflow_table_entries went on serving its 7 rows with meta exactly
  {"dpid": 6} at +2 s and +6 s -- no stale_since, no stale_polls, no last_error -- and only at
  +12 s, after the first failed poll, did the markers appear. "The switch said this" and "we have
  not asked since it died" are indistinguishable inside that window. After it, the marking is
  correct and the cache self-corrects (0 rows at readopt+5 s, back to 4 at +25 s).

N4 (from rank 3). RULES AN OPERATOR INSTALLED ARE LOST BY A POWER CYCLE AND NEVER RESTORED, WHILE
  THE DISPATCH RECORD STILL SAYS THEY SUCCEEDED. MEDIUM. 05_.
  Three rules installed on s6 (present in BOTH channels: kernel cache and the proxy's P4Runtime
  read, 7 rows each). Power-cycle + readopt: readopt answers `routes_installed: 0`, the link
  watchdog restores the FOUR boot routes at +12 s, and 10.0.0.231/232/233 are gone from both
  channels permanently. /ndt/get_flow_dispatch_status still counts them succeeded. Nothing warns.
  May be intended (a bmv2 restart loses everything) -- but the twin's own success record does not
  expire with it, so from outside the operator cannot tell.

N5 (from rank 1). THE ELEPHANT-FLOW CLASSIFICATION IS WRITE-ONLY. LOW. 03_.
  isElephantFlowPeriodically / isElephantFlowImmediately are declared once
  (include/common_types/SFlowType.hpp:665-666), assigned at six sites in FlowLinkUsageCollector.cpp
  (:2004 :2022 :2026 :2243 :2251 :2255) and READ AT NO SITE in src/ include/ tools/ tests/ p4_proxy/.
  They appear in none of the 15 keys /ndt/get_detected_top_k_flow_data ever returned across 76
  polls. Consequence for triage: COMMON-BRIEF s11's worry that the no-denominator bias "feeds
  top-k ordering AND elephant-flow classification" is half true. It feeds the ordering (the sort
  key at FlowLinkUsageCollector.cpp:2530 is estimated_packet_rate_in_the_proceeding_1sec_timeslot).
  It feeds no elephant decision, because nothing reads the flag.

