C. REFUTED -- doors taken off the list (worth as much)
================================================================================
rank 1. The archaeology said the "Immediately" variant at :2020-2027 might be the unfixed twin of
  the fixed one. It is the other way round: :2020-2027 IS the fixed Periodically variant, and the
  Immediately variant at :2243-2255 has BOTH guards -- the `!hasActiveHops` clear and the `else
  ... = false`. Live: 10 s of 50 Mbit/s UDP (5x the 10 Mbit/s MICE_FLOW_UNDER_THRESHOLD,
  TopologyAndFlowMonitor.hpp:30), then 28 s idle, polled at 2 Hz. Rate fields fell to 0 within
  ~1.5 s of the flow stopping and the row walked active -> idle (t+13) -> ended (t+25) -> gone
  (t+25.7). A-3's fix is good live. And the stated observable cannot exist at all -- see N5.

rank 2. Per-switch epoch consistency is correct. In 09_ phase C the six healthy switches were swept
  to the new 3-rule snapshot in the same rounds in which the three failing ones kept their old 4,
  and the empty-array switch was swept to 0. No cross-contamination in either direction; the
  concern that "only the empty-array switch gets swept while rules from another switch survive"
  is exactly what the code does on purpose, per switch, and it recovers within 15 s.

rank 3. `m_cachedOpenFlowTables` is refreshed by a ~10 s worker and self-corrects; it is not
  "written once in applyFetchedTables". Two directions measured (05_): stale-high for <=12 s
  after a switch dies, stale-low for ~13 s after readopt empties it. And `POST /p4/readopt/{dpid}`
  cannot even be used as the archaeology's trigger on a live adopted switch: it answers
  502 {"step":"mastership", "...The switch was not touched."} (04_).

rank 4. On the P4 plane the window does not exist. The code at
  DeviceConfigurationAndPowerManager.cpp:730-745 is the BMV2 branch (not, as the archaeology says,
  an OvsLiveness worker -- OvsLiveness is the enum type), and it runs only if fetchP4SwitchState
  succeeded. Own evidence, third independent confirmation of round 2 D15 / round 3 lead C:
  0 `GET /p4/switch_state` requests in 1929 proxy log lines, against 108 `/v1.0/topology` polls,
  and 0 `cannot read bmv2 liveness` warnings in kernel.log -- so it never tried and never failed.
  p4LivenessFor always receives nullopt, the switch(){} is never entered, and there is no
  probe-to-setVertexUp window to lose. The OVS branch is different code and remains untested.

rank 5. Live on P4: install_group_entry, modify_group_entry and delete_group_entry all answer
  HTTP 501 with a precise body and outcome "unsupported_on_p4" (06_). The OpResult-discarded
  pattern the archaeology cites was already fixed (HttpSession.cpp:996 now passes the OpResult to
  respondToOpResult). Distinct from round 1's D5, which is the OVS delete path.

rank 6. P4 arm refuted the same way: install/modify/delete_meter_entry all 501 (07_). The claim
  lives in HttpRoutingStrategyBase (the OVS/HTTP path) and is NOT-SETTLED -- see D.

rank 7. Refuted as a cache defect and attributed instead. /ndt/get_power_report returned dpid 8 =
  147622 mW unchanged 3/12/25/45 s after s8's process was provably gone (ps on the exact pid 0
  rows, :30058 closed). But the value never depended on state in the first place: round 2 D14
  showed it is splitmix64 of the dpid. The stale number is D14, and "a dead switch still reads ON"
  is round 2 B2/B3. There is no separate cache bug here. (cpu/memory both return the F-1 sentinel
  -1 for all ten; power still has no sentinel and no `source` field -- also D14.)

rank 9. Same defect as round 1 D5. Not re-run, not re-reported.

