D. NOT DONE, AND WHY
================================================================================
rank 6 OVS arm | Needs an OVS fabric. `modifyAnEntry` extracts a priority (HttpRoutingStrategyBase
                 .cpp:295-300) that cannot identify a meter, and only the OVS/HTTP path reaches it.
                 The experiment is: install meter 88, then POST modify_meter_entry for meter_id 88
                 with a priority no meter was created with, and read the meter back from Ryu's own
                 /stats/meterconfig (never the API that wrote it). Round 1 proved that read-back
                 channel works (D5's positive control). Round 5's R1 predicts "accepted, logged,
                 ignored"; nobody has run it. ~20 min on an ovs4 fabric.
rank 4 OVS arm | Same fabric, and it is a race: it needs power churn plus load, and round 2 D17
                 already measured the analogous poll-vs-command race on P4 at 8/14. Lower value
                 than rank 6 for the same bring-up cost.
Both were dropped because an ovs4 bring-up plus teardown would not have left time to verify the
lab clean before the deadline, and round 1 measured that bring-up at tens of minutes.

================================================================================
E. FOR A HUMAN TO DECIDE
================================================================================
1. N1 is the one to fix, and it is small: `noteAndAnnouncePollRound` needs the fetch OUTCOME, not
   the body string. The same binary already does it correctly one module over
   (last_error=reported_failure / unparseable on the flow-table fetch); copying that distinction
   into the topology poll is the whole change. Until then, `topology-round-partial` detects a
   timeout and nothing else, and every "the twin agrees with the fabric" check that relies on it
   is blind to a controller that answers 500.
2. N2: pick one behaviour for "the switch was not read". Today an HTTP error and a non-JSON body
   both keep the last snapshot and say so; a well-formed body of the wrong TYPE is republished raw
   with no marker. The last one is the only one an API consumer cannot defend against.
3. N5: either read the elephant flags or delete them. They have been computed every second, in two
   variants, through at least three bug reports about their clearing semantics, and nothing has
   ever consumed them.
4. N4: decide whether the twin should reconcile operator-installed rules after a power cycle, or
   whether /ndt/get_flow_dispatch_status should stop implying they still exist.

